# Modular equation Φ_ℓ(j(qᵈ),j(q^{dℓ}))=0 over any base ring

Theorem statement: `ModularCurve.ModularPolynomialData.eval_jqNModC_mul_eq_zero`
(`ab724e6a-4529-5d24-9a04-8ca86401ed38`), by Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `06a8b185-979e-5851-afda-578c5c1f8736`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Let $\ell \ge 1$ and let `data` be a modular-polynomial datum of level $\ell$, that is, a
two-variable integral polynomial $\Phi =$ `data.Φ` $\in (\mathbb{Z}[X])[Y]$ which is monic in $Y$,
whose degree in $Y$ equals $\psi(\ell) = \sum_{d \mid \ell,\ d \text{ squarefree}} \ell/d$, and
which satisfies $\Phi(j(q), j(q^{\ell})) = 0$ in the field of rational Laurent series
$\mathbb{Q}((q))$, the evaluation being: coefficients in $\mathbb{Z}[X]$ sent to $\mathbb{Q}((q))$
by $X \mapsto$ the $q$-expansion $j(q)$, and $Y \mapsto$ its image under the exponent-scaling ring
endomorphism $q \mapsto q^{\ell}$. Let $K$ be any commutative ring and $d \ge 1$. Write `jqNModC K
d` for the Laurent series over $K$ obtained from $q^{-1}$ times the image in $K[[q]]$ of the
integral power series part of $j$ by multiplying all exponents by $d$, i.e. for the reduction to $K$
of the $q$-expansion of $j(q^{d})$. Then $\Phi$ evaluated with coefficients in $\mathbb{Z}[X]$
mapped to $K((q))$ by $X \mapsto$ `jqNModC K d` and with $Y \mapsto$ `jqNModC K (d * ℓ)` is $0$ in
$K((q))$.

This is the classical modular equation $\Phi_\ell(j(q^{d}), j(q^{d\ell})) = 0$, transported from its
defining identity over $\mathbb{Q}$ to the Laurent series ring over an arbitrary commutative ring
$K$ and to an arbitrary scaling $q \mapsto q^{d}$ of the variable. It is the basic algebraic
relation used throughout the treatment of modular curves in characteristic $p$, for instance in
computing the relative degree of $K(j(q^{d}))$-extensions generated by $j(q^{d\ell})$ and in the
localisation arguments for the reduction of $X_0$.

